.TH SYS_CPU_COUNT 2 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
sys_cpu_count \- get number of CPUs in the system
.SH LIBRARY
System library (libsys, \-lsys)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/syscall.h>
.PP
.BI "int sys_cpu_count(void);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B sys_cpu_count()
system call returns the number of CPUs (processors) available in the system.
.PP
On uniprocessor systems, this always returns 1.
On SMP (Symmetric Multi-Processing) systems, this returns the number of
online CPUs.
.PP
This call is useful for applications that need to tune their behavior
based on available parallelism, such as thread pool sizing.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, returns the number of CPUs (always >= 1).
.PP
This function does not fail under normal circumstances.
.SH ERRORS
This system call does not return any errors.
.SH NOTES
The current implementation returns 1 as the kernel is uniprocessor.
Future SMP support will return the actual online CPU count.
.PP
This differs from
.BR sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN)
in that it is a direct system call rather than a library interface.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_proc_count (2),
.BR uname (2),
.BR intro (2)
